1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,399
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work, from AWS AI and UC Santa Barbara,</font>

2
00:00:03,479 --> 00:00:09,762
<font color="#FFFFFF">is titled "Automatic Clipping: Differentially
Private Deep Learning Made Easier and Stronger."</font>

3
00:00:09,842 --> 00:00:15,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">Differentially private training of deep networks
relies on per-sample gradient clipping,</font>

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:22,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the clipping threshold R is a fragile
hyperparameter that must be tuned carefully for good accuracy.</font>

5
00:00:22,841 --> 00:00:25,401
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors propose automatic clipping,</font>

6
00:00:25,481 --> 00:00:30,005
<font color="#FFFFFF">a drop-in replacement that removes R entirely from
any DP optimizer,</font>

7
00:00:30,085 --> 00:00:37,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">so private training becomes as tuning-friendly as
ordinary training while matching or beating the state of the art.</font>

8
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:42,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">In differentially private deep learning,</font>

9
00:00:42,607 --> 00:00:46,964
<font color="#FFFFFF">every per-sample gradient is clipped to a fixed
norm R before noise is added,</font>

10
00:00:47,044 --> 00:00:51,401
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that single threshold R turns out to be
decisive for accuracy.</font>

11
00:00:51,481 --> 00:00:55,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">Picking it wrong is costly: on ImageNet,</font>

12
00:00:55,919 --> 00:01:00,276
<font color="#FFFFFF">ResNet18 accuracy can collapse from forty-five
percent to thirty-one percent when R is merely doubled.</font>

13
00:01:00,356 --> 00:01:04,713
<font color="#FFFFFF">State-of-the-art private models tend to need very
small clipping thresholds that can only be found through careful,</font>

14
00:01:04,793 --> 00:01:09,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">expensive tuning.</font>

15
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:14,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">Searching jointly over the clipping threshold and
the learning rate is one of the main reasons DP training is painful.</font>

16
00:01:14,618 --> 00:01:19,627
<font color="#FFFFFF">For large models this grid search can take days to
months of compute,</font>

17
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:24,715
<font color="#FFFFFF">and because it inspects private data it also spends
additional privacy budget.</font>

18
00:01:24,795 --> 00:01:29,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

19
00:01:29,883 --> 00:01:34,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">the best thresholds are usually so small that
nearly every per-sample gradient is clipped at every step,</font>

20
00:01:34,972 --> 00:01:39,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">which hints that the precise value of R may not
matter at all if we reformulate the clipping.</font>

21
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:44,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors make four contributions.</font>

22
00:01:44,673 --> 00:01:48,906
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, they propose automatic clipping,</font>

23
00:01:48,986 --> 00:01:53,219
<font color="#FFFFFF">which mathematically expunges the clipping
threshold from general DP optimizers including DP-SGD,</font>

24
00:01:53,299 --> 00:01:57,532
<font color="#FFFFFF">DP-Adam, and DP-LAMB.</font>

25
00:01:57,612 --> 00:02:01,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

26
00:02:01,925 --> 00:02:06,158
<font color="#FFFFFF">they prove that automatic DP-SGD converges in the
non-convex setting at the same asymptotic rate as standard SGD.</font>

27
00:02:06,238 --> 00:02:10,471
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, they show any positive constant threshold is
equivalent to setting it to one,</font>

28
00:02:10,551 --> 00:02:14,784
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a single default suffices.</font>

29
00:02:14,864 --> 00:02:19,097
<font color="#FFFFFF">And fourth, they demonstrate superior results
across vision and language benchmarks,</font>

30
00:02:19,177 --> 00:02:23,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">achievable by changing a single line of code in
popular libraries.</font>

31
00:02:23,790 --> 00:02:28,762
<font color="#FFFFFF">The idea starts from a simple observation: when the
threshold is small,</font>

32
00:02:28,842 --> 00:02:33,956
<font color="#FFFFFF">Abadi's clipping factor, the minimum of R over the
gradient norm and one,</font>

33
00:02:34,036 --> 00:02:37,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">is almost always just R over the gradient norm.</font>

34
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:43,063
<font color="#FFFFFF">So the authors drop the minimum entirely and
normalize every per-sample gradient,</font>

35
00:02:43,143 --> 00:02:45,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">a variant they call AUTO-V for vanilla.</font>

36
00:02:45,918 --> 00:02:50,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">This maximizes alignment between the private and
true gradient,</font>

37
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:56,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it makes all gradients the same size, creating
a lazy region where updates stall.</font>

38
00:02:56,448 --> 00:03:01,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">To fix this they add a small stability constant
gamma in the denominator,</font>

39
00:03:01,642 --> 00:03:05,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving AUTO-S: R divided by the gradient norm plus gamma.</font>

40
00:03:05,697 --> 00:03:11,238
<font color="#FFFFFF">This preserves relative magnitudes, letting small
gradients shrink toward zero.</font>

41
00:03:11,318 --> 00:03:16,503
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally, because any constant threshold simply
rescales the learning rate,</font>

42
00:03:16,583 --> 00:03:21,413
<font color="#FFFFFF">they fix R to one, and set gamma to a default of
zero point zero one,</font>

43
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:24,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">leaving a fully threshold-free optimizer.</font>

44
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:27,119
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is tested broadly.</font>

45
00:03:27,199 --> 00:03:34,414
<font color="#FFFFFF">On language, the authors finetune RoBERTa base and
large on the GLUE tasks MNLI, QQP,</font>

46
00:03:34,494 --> 00:03:35,787
<font color="#FFFFFF">QNLI, and SST-2,</font>

47
00:03:35,867 --> 00:03:43,597
<font color="#FFFFFF">and finetune GPT2 in three sizes for table-to-text
generation on the E2E and DART datasets.</font>

48
00:03:43,677 --> 00:03:48,832
<font color="#FFFFFF">On vision, they evaluate CIFAR-10 with a pretrained
SimCLRv2,</font>

49
00:03:48,912 --> 00:03:53,467
<font color="#FFFFFF">ImageNette with a ResNet9, and ImageNet with ResNet18.</font>

50
00:03:53,547 --> 00:04:01,019
<font color="#FFFFFF">They use standard privacy budgets of epsilon three
and epsilon eight and reuse the exact</font>

51
00:04:01,099 --> 00:04:05,053
<font color="#FFFFFF">hyperparameters of prior state-of-the-art work,</font>

52
00:04:05,133 --> 00:04:07,370
<font color="#FFFFFF">changing only the clipping.</font>

53
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:14,058
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across the board, automatic clipping matches or
outperforms the state of the art,</font>

54
00:04:14,138 --> 00:04:17,922
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it does so without ever tuning the threshold.</font>

55
00:04:18,002 --> 00:04:20,446
<font color="#FFFFFF">For GPT2 text generation on E2E,</font>

56
00:04:20,526 --> 00:04:26,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">AUTO-S reaches a BLEU score of sixty-four point one
eight at epsilon three,</font>

57
00:04:26,441 --> 00:04:31,013
<font color="#FFFFFF">edging past the prior best of sixty-three point
eight five.</font>

58
00:04:31,093 --> 00:04:36,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">For RoBERTa-base on SST-2, it reaches ninety-two
point three two percent,</font>

59
00:04:36,850 --> 00:04:40,162
<font color="#FFFFFF">above the prior ninety-one point eight six.</font>

60
00:04:40,242 --> 00:04:47,496
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-10 with a pretrained SimCLRv2 it hits
ninety-two point seven percent at epsilon two.</font>

61
00:04:47,576 --> 00:04:51,518
<font color="#FFFFFF">And because only the learning rate needs searching,</font>

62
00:04:51,598 --> 00:04:54,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">the tuning cost drops by about five times.</font>

63
00:04:55,210 --> 00:04:59,109
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two ablations anchor the design.</font>

64
00:04:59,189 --> 00:05:03,088
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

65
00:05:03,168 --> 00:05:07,067
<font color="#FFFFFF">comparing AUTO-V and AUTO-S shows that once the
small stability constant restores gradient magnitude,</font>

66
00:05:07,147 --> 00:05:11,046
<font color="#FFFFFF">AUTO-S consistently wins, confirming that the lazy
region really does hurt AUTO-V.</font>

67
00:05:11,126 --> 00:05:15,025
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

68
00:05:15,105 --> 00:05:19,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">sweeping the stability constant gamma shows the
method is essentially insensitive to it:</font>

69
00:05:19,084 --> 00:05:22,983
<font color="#FFFFFF">any positive gamma gives the same asymptotic
convergence rate,</font>

70
00:05:23,063 --> 00:05:26,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is why a single default of zero point zero
one works everywhere.</font>

71
00:05:27,042 --> 00:05:30,941
<font color="#FFFFFF">Heatmaps over threshold and learning rate further
show the AUTO-S result landing right</font>

72
00:05:31,021 --> 00:05:34,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">at the best hand-tuned threshold.</font>

73
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:40,236
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline numbers: GPT2 on E2E reaches BLEU
sixty-four point one eight at epsilon three,</font>

74
00:05:40,316 --> 00:05:45,252
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus sixty-three point eight five for the prior best.</font>

75
00:05:45,332 --> 00:05:50,268
<font color="#FFFFFF">RoBERTa-base on SST-2 reaches ninety-two point
three two percent,</font>

76
00:05:50,348 --> 00:05:55,284
<font color="#FFFFFF">and RoBERTa-large on the same task reaches
ninety-four point six one percent at epsilon eight.</font>

77
00:05:55,364 --> 00:06:00,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">CIFAR-10 with SimCLRv2 hits ninety-two point seven
percent at epsilon two.</font>

78
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:05,316
<font color="#FFFFFF">The sensitivity of the old approach is stark: on ImageNet,</font>

79
00:06:05,396 --> 00:06:10,332
<font color="#FFFFFF">doubling the threshold drops ResNet18 accuracy from
forty-five to thirty-one percent.</font>

80
00:06:10,412 --> 00:06:15,348
<font color="#FFFFFF">Theoretically,</font>

81
00:06:15,428 --> 00:06:20,364
<font color="#FFFFFF">the minimum expected gradient norm shrinks at the
rate T to the minus one quarter,</font>

82
00:06:20,444 --> 00:06:25,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">matching standard SGD.</font>

83
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:29,204
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is that the clipping threshold,</font>

84
00:06:29,284 --> 00:06:33,833
<font color="#FFFFFF">long treated as a critical knob in differentially
private training,</font>

85
00:06:33,913 --> 00:06:35,353
<font color="#FFFFFF">can simply be removed.</font>

86
00:06:35,433 --> 00:06:40,674
<font color="#FFFFFF">By normalizing each per-sample gradient and adding
a tiny stability constant,</font>

87
00:06:40,754 --> 00:06:44,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">you get an optimizer that is just as private, just
as fast,</font>

88
00:06:44,830 --> 00:06:48,343
<font color="#FFFFFF">and just as accurate as the best hand-tuned methods,</font>

89
00:06:48,423 --> 00:06:52,212
<font color="#FFFFFF">backed by a convergence guarantee matching standard SGD.</font>

90
00:06:52,292 --> 00:06:55,391
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is a one-line change in existing libraries,</font>

91
00:06:55,471 --> 00:07:00,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">which finally makes DP training about as easy as
ordinary training.</font>
